+# CSV contact import
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and today has to add them to contactbook one by one.
+They want to load a whole CSV file at once and then see, row by row, what was created,
+what was updated and what needs fixing. Problem rows should be reported with the row
+number they see in the spreadsheet. Everything else in the file should still go in.
+
+The file has the same columns as our CSV export (`export_csv`: `name,email,phone,tags`,
+with tags joined by `;`). It may also be written by Excel ("CSV UTF-8") or by our
+address-book export script, which writes `first_name` and `last_name` instead of `name`.
+
+## Interface
+
+Fixed. Other code calls exactly this:
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Row number**: the row number Sales sees in a spreadsheet. The header is row 1 and the
+  first contact is row 2. Every row counts, blank rows included. A quoted cell containing a
+  line break does not start a new row. Without such cells, row N is line N of the file.
+- **Blank row**: a row where every cell is empty or whitespace only.
+- **Contact row**: any row after the header that is not blank, whether or not it turns out
+  to be valid.
+- **Accepted row**: a contact row that produced no error. Accepted rows are applied to the
+  repository.
+- **`column`** in a `RowIssue` is one of `"name"`, `"email"`, `"phone"`, `"tags"`, or `None`
+  for problems that don't belong to one of those columns.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Decoding
+
+1. Bytes are decoded as UTF-8. No other encoding is attempted.
+2. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 raise `ImportFileError`. The message tells the user to
+   re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8".
+3. A leading UTF-8 BOM is ignored. This applies to bytes starting with the BOM and to text
+   starting with the BOM character (`\ufeff`). Such a file gives the same result as the
+   same file without the BOM, and in particular its first header column is recognised.
+4. Text input (`str`) is used as is, apart from the BOM rule above.
+5. The delimiter is a comma, and standard CSV quoting is used (as written by `export_csv`
+   and Excel). LF and CRLF line endings are both accepted.
+
+### Header
+
+6. The first row is the header. If the file is empty or the first row is blank,
+   `ImportFileError` is raised.
+7. Header names are matched case-insensitively with surrounding whitespace ignored.
+   Columns may appear in any order.
+8. An `email` column is required. If it is missing, `ImportFileError` is raised.
+9. The name must come from either a `name` column or both a `first_name` and a `last_name`
+   column. If neither form is present, `ImportFileError` is raised. Having only one of
+   `first_name`/`last_name` counts as having neither.
+10. If `name` is present, it is used. Any `first_name`/`last_name` columns are then
+    treated as unknown columns.
+11. `phone` and `tags` columns are optional.
+12. Unknown columns never cause an error. Their values are ignored. Each unknown column
+    produces one warning with `row=1`, `column=None` and the column's header text in the
+    message.
+13. If any of `name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`, `first_name` or `last_name` appears more
+    than once in the header, `ImportFileError` is raised. Repeated unknown columns are
+    allowed.
+14. A header with no contact rows after it is not an error. It returns an empty report
+    and leaves the repository unchanged.
+
+### File size
+
+15. A file with more than 1000 contact rows raises `ImportFileError`.
+16. A file with exactly 1000 contact rows is accepted. Blank rows don't count towards the
+    limit.
+
+### File-level failure
+
+17. When `ImportFileError` is raised, the repository is exactly as it was before the call.
+    Nothing has been created or updated.
+18. `ImportFileError` is the only domain error that `import_contacts` raises. Row-level
+    validation problems are reported in the `ImportReport`. Passing `data` that is neither
+    `str` nor `bytes` is a programming error, not an `ImportFileError`.
+
+### Reading a row
+
+19. Blank rows are skipped silently. They produce no issue and appear in no list, but they
+    still count in row numbering.
+20. If a row has fewer cells than the header, the missing trailing cells are treated as
+    empty.
+21. A row with more cells than the header is an error with `column=None` and is not
+    applied.
+22. All values are validated and normalised by the package's existing rules, the same as
+    `Contact.create`. The repository only ever receives normalised contacts.
+23. **Name from `name`**: the cell value, validated as a name.
+24. **Name from `first_name` + `last_name`**: the two cells joined with one space. If one
+    of them is blank, the name is the other one alone, with no stray space. If both are
+    blank, the row is a name error. All name problems from this form are reported with
+    `column="name"`.
+25. **Tags**: the cell is split on `;`. Surrounding whitespace on each piece is ignored.
+    Empty or whitespace-only pieces are ignored, and repeated tags collapse to one. A cell
+    that is empty or contains only separators means "no tags in this row".
+
+### Row errors (row skipped)
+
+26. Each of these makes the row an error:
+    - an email that is missing or invalid (`column="email"`)
+    - a name that is missing or invalid (`column="name"`)
+    - any invalid tag (`column="tags"`)
+    - too many cells (`column=None`, see 21)
+    - a duplicate email (`column="email"`, see 31)
+27. A row with an error is not applied at all. A new contact is not created, and an
+    existing contact is not changed in any field, not even partly.
+28. Each erroneous row has at least one `RowIssue` in `errors` with that row's number.
+    Whether a row with several problems gets one issue or several is the implementer's
+    choice.
+29. One erroneous row does not stop the other rows from being imported.
+
+### Duplicates within the file
+
+30. Emails are compared after normalisation, so `Ann@X.com` and ` ann@x.com ` are the
+    same email.
+31. Once a row with a given email has been accepted, every later row with that email is
+    an error on the later row (`column="email"`) and is not applied. This holds whether
+    the accepted row created, updated, or left the contact unchanged.
+32. An erroneous row does not claim its email. A later valid row with the same email is
+    treated as if the erroneous row weren't there.
+
+### Phone (warning, never an error)
+
+33. An invalid phone value does not make the row an error. The row gets a warning with
+    `column="phone"`, and the rest of the row is applied as if the phone cell were empty.
+34. An empty phone cell, a missing `phone` column, or an invalid phone never removes or
+    changes a phone that an existing contact already has.
+
+### New contacts (`created`)
+
+35. An accepted row whose email is not in the repository creates a contact with these
+    fields:
+    - the row's name and email
+    - the row's phone, if the phone is valid; otherwise no phone
+    - the row's tags
+36. Its normalised email is appended to `created`.
+
+### Existing contacts (`updated`)
+
+37. An accepted row whose email is already in the repository changes the stored contact
+    as follows. No other changes are made.
+    - **Name**: replaced by the row's name.
+    - **Phone**: replaced only if the row has a valid phone value (see 33–34).
+    - **Tags**: the row's tags are added to the existing tags. An import never removes a
+      tag.
+38. If that changes the stored contact, its normalised email is appended to `updated`.
+39. If the stored contact would end up identical (same name, same phone, no new tags),
+    the email appears in neither `created` nor `updated`. Any phone warning for the row
+    is still reported.
+
+### Report
+
+40. `created` and `updated` list normalised emails in file order. No email appears twice
+    in a list, and no email appears in both lists.
+41. `errors` and `warnings` hold `RowIssue`s with the row numbers defined above. Messages
+    are short and human-readable.
+42. Every contact row is accounted for in one of these ways:
+    - its email is in `created` or `updated`
+    - it is an accepted row that changed nothing
+    - it has at least one entry in `errors`
+43. **Round trip**: importing the output of `export_csv(contacts)` into an empty
+    repository creates exactly those contacts, with the same field values in the same
+    order. The report has no errors and no warnings.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Encodings other than UTF-8, including a fallback to Windows-1252 or Latin-1.
+- Delimiters other than comma, including detecting `;`-delimited files from European
+  Excel locales.
+- Deleting contacts, clearing phones, or removing tags through an import.
+- A dry-run or preview mode, or all-or-nothing import of rows. Bad rows are skipped and
+  good rows go in.
+- Importing columns the contact model doesn't have. Unknown columns are ignored.
+- Files with more than 1000 contact rows, streaming, or progress reporting.
+- Changing the CSV export, for example to write `first_name`/`last_name`.
+- Concurrency: the repository is not thread-safe, and the import doesn't change that.
